s='''<nav><a href="/">← Aleph</a></nav><section class="hero"><p class="eyebrow">DSA · Three-week review</p><h1>Represent it. Preserve it. Count it.</h1><p class="sub">A Skiena-based review selected for GATE DA, with CMI MSc Data Science pseudocode reasoning where it overlaps. Week 1 contains seven complete review-and-diagnostic lessons.</p><p>Edition: The Algorithm Design Manual, third edition. Relative study days; no calendar dates assigned.</p></section><div class="phase-grid"><section><h2>Week 1 · Refresh and diagnose</h2><p>Days1–7 cover the shared core. Each day has review exposition, two worked examples, a five-question diagnostic, worked feedback, a repair prompt, a fresh check, a big-picture summary and a mind map. Available now.</p></section><section id="week2"><h2>Week 2 · Mixed applications</h2><p>Days8–14 revisit the same scope through new inputs, implementation comparisons and compact exam-style combinations. Use Week1 evidence to adjust emphasis. Daily material not yet authored.</p></section><section><h2>Week 3 · Review and remedy</h2><p>Days15–21 target confirmed errors, then use delayed independent checks and a mixed final review. Daily material will be selected from the prior diagnostics; it is not yet authored.</p></section></div><section class="panel"><h2>What belongs in the intersection?</h2><p>Skiena is broader than the exam. The core reading comes from analysis, elementary structures, sorting, hashing, traversal and weighted paths. The official CMI MSc Data Science entrance syllabus asks for simple pseudocode interpretation rather than explicitly listing the entire GATE data-structures curriculum. We therefore label CMI-oriented trace questions separately instead of claiming that every advanced structure is an explicit CMI topic.</p><p>The programming supplement supplies Python-specific behavior required by GATE DA. All exposition and problems here are original reading companions, not copied book exercises or claimed past examination questions.</p><div class="overlap"><table><thead><tr><th>Topic</th><th>Skiena reading anchor</th><th>Exam relationship</th><th>Days</th></tr></thead><tbody>'''
for row in rows:s+='<tr>'+''.join('<td>'+E(x)+'</td>' for x in row)+'</tr>'
s+='''</tbody></table></div></section><section class="panel"><h2>Seven-day review route</h2><ol class="toc">'''
